Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Symbolic Computing\n\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["LT20, 4:00-6:00pm\nWed, 8 April 2003",
    FontSize->24,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Title"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Revision class",
  FontFamily->"Arial Black"]], "Subtitle"],

Cell["Part I, Mathematica", "Subtitle"],

Cell["1. Basic Syntax", "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We expect a mastery of the basic syntax of ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". In particular, we want to be familiar with many of the special symbols \
and notations in Mathematica, such as:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
=\tSet (assignment)
:=\tdelayed Set (delayed assignment)
->\treplacement rule
/.\tapply (of a rule)
//.\tapply repeated
@@\tthe Apply[  ] function
/@\tMap[ ] function
//\tpostfix (of function)
@\tprefix (of function)
~f~\tinfix (of function)
f[#]&\tpure function
x_\tpattern (anything named x)
x__\tone or more 
x___\tzero or more
{  }\tList
(  )\tgrouping
[  ]\tfunction argument
[[  ]]\tindexing
(*  *)\tcomments
%\tprevious output
==\tcompare for equal
===\tcompare for identical (stronger)
,\tcomma separate arguments 
;\tgroup expressions as logically one

etc.\
\>", "Theorem"],

Cell["2. Principles of Mathematica", "Subsubtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " follows few well-defined principles, that we try to summarize here:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(a)  Do exact calculation unless input is approximate.  E.g.,\
\>", "Theorem"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Sqrt[3], \ Sqrt[3.0]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\@3, 1.7320508075688772`}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
(b)  Apply built-in and user-defined rules until results no longer change \
(thus recursive).  Recognize the rules (or definitions) according to pattern \
matching.\
\>", "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "(c) Uniform internal data and program format (expressions) :\n             \
   ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`h\)]],
  "[",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\_1\)]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\_2\)]],
  ", ..., ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\_n\)]],
  "]\nwhere ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`h\)]],
  " is the head, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\_i\)]],
  " is its ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`i\)]],
  "th argument.     \nA collection of list manipulation functions (such as \
Take[ ], Drop[ ], Union[ ], Join[ ])\nwork on any expression, as well as \
special important type List {  }.           "
}], "Theorem"],

Cell["\<\
For example,  {a,b,c} (same as List[a,b,c]) can be used as array storing \
data, or matrix {{a,b},{c,d}}.  But the program 
f[x_] := Module[{}, ...] is also in the same format h[a,b,c].\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["3. Programming Style", "Subsubtitle"],

Cell["\<\
We expect students can program in several different programing styles:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(A) Procedural programing style using explicit loop control such as Do[  ], \
While[  ], For[  ], If[  ], Return[  ],  Goto[ ], etc.\
\>", "Theorem"],

Cell["\<\
A procedural based program normally is enclosed in a Module[ ].  E.g.:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(myprog[arg1_, \ arg2_]\  := \ 
      Module[{i, local1, \ 
          local2, \  ... }, \[IndentingNewLine]\(\(...\)\(;\)\(\
\[IndentingNewLine]\)\(For[i\  = \ 0, \ 
            i\  < \ 10, \ \(++i\), \[IndentingNewLine]Print[\  ... ]; \
\[IndentingNewLine]do\ \
that;\[IndentingNewLine]]\)\(;\)\(\[IndentingNewLine]\)\(returnvalue\)\)\
\[IndentingNewLine]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Note that you cannot reassign values to argument variables.", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
(B) Rule-base programming defines a set of transformation rules using \
patterns.  The same name can be given several distinct definitions, the use \
of which is decided by pattern matching.   In a rule-base programming, the \
left-hand side of the delayed assignment (the pattern) can be complicated.\
\>", "Theorem"],

Cell["\<\
Here is an example of rule base programming for counting the number of \
arguments:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(argn[]\  := \ 0;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(\(argn[x_]\  := \ 1;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(argn[x_, \ y__]\  := \ 1\  + \ argn[y]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
(C)  Functional programing style avoids side effect (such as looping).  \
Functional programing uses Map[ ], Apply[ ], Nest[ ], Fold[ ], and pure \
functions to get the effect of looping.\
\>", "Theorem"],

Cell["\<\
The question of when to use a pure function is dictated by syntax.  Suppose \
we need to square each element in a list. With Map, we must use pure \
function:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[#^2 &, \ {a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a\^2, b\^2, c\^2}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
or we can define a square function first.  Note that we use square without \
argument. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(square[x_] := \ x^2;\)\), "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    \(Map[square, \ {a, b, c}]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a\^2, b\^2, c\^2}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Equivalently:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[square[#] &, \ {a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a\^2, b\^2, c\^2}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["What to review?", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
(1) One can do the lab problems again (independently, in an examination \
setting)
(2) Review the tutorial problems
(3) Reading
(4) Do the following problems\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Exercise Problems:"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "(1) Tell us exactly what is ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica'",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "s response with the following inputs (assuming each one is independent of \
others):  [For correct answer, you should do a Clear[variable] for these \
expressions that produce side affect, such as assigning values to variables]"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((a + b*c)\)[\([2, 2]\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x = 1; \ y = 2 + x; \ x = 2; y\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\  @@ \ {a, b}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[f, g[a, b]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[f[f[x]]]\  //. \ f[x]\  \[Rule] x\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Fold[f, x, {a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, 2, 3}^3\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[x] := x^2; f[y_] = y^3; \ f[x] + f[y]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Sqrt[#] &\)\ [4]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[f[a^x], \ f[x^x_]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[I]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a + b*c\  /. \ Plus\  \[Rule] \ List\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a*b*c\  //. \ x_*\ y_\  \[Rule] \ x + y\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Part[Sin[x*y + z], 1, 2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(List\  @@ \ f[a, b, c]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\({x \[Rule] #1} &\)\  /@ \ {a, b, c}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[f[a, b, c], f[x_]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Drop[{a, b, c}^3, 1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[Real]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, {b, c}} = {1, {2, 3}}; \ b\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a \((a)\), \ a {a}, \ a[a]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(#^2 &\)[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = Sin; \ a[Pi]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1, 2, 3}\  // f\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\  @@ \ {1, 2, 3}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FullForm[x^3 + u*y]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  //. \ x \[Rule] \ x\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Log\  /@ \ {E, \ E^2, \ 1}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sum[1, {5}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ReplacePart[{{1, 0}, {0, 1}}, a, {2, 2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Map[#^3 &, x + y + 2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Function[var, var^2 + 1]\)[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Apply[Times, {1, 2, 3, 4}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Select[{1, a, x^2, 3, 5, 1 + x, 7}, \ # < 4 &]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[E]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\({x \[Rule] #[\([1]\)], y \[Rule] #[\([2]\)]}\  &\)\  /@ \ {{1, 
          2}, {3, 4}}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  // \ f[#] &\)], "Input"],

Cell["(2) What do the following programs do?", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(lglg[f_, {x_, xmin_, xmax_}, opts___] := \ 
      ParametricPlot[{Log[10, x], Log[10, f]}, {x, xmin, xmax}, 
        opts]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(super[lis_List] := 
      lis\  //. \ {x___, a_, b_, y___}\  /; \ 
            b\  \[LessEqual] a\  \[Rule] \ {x, a, y}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(prog[xlist_List] := \ 
      Module[{elements = Union[xlist]}, 
        Map[{#, Count[xlist, #]} &, elements]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(myproc[n_] := \ 
      Module[{x = 0, z = 1}, 
        If[n \[Equal] 0, 1, \ While[x \[NotEqual] n, \(++x\); z = z*x]]; 
        z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Revision class",
    FontFamily->"Arial Black"],
  "\n\nPart II, computer algebra theory"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell["Definition of algebraic domains", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
We expect the students to know the definitions of group, abelian group, ring, \
integral domain, Euclidean domain, and field.
All of them should be closed (axiom A0) with respect to one or two \
operations. Roughly speaking, the algebraic domains are the following:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "GROUP:  \nIf ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\)]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\)]],
  " \[Element] G, so is group multiplication ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ b\)]],
  " \[Element] G.\n(1) ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(\ \)\(a\ \((b\ c)\)\  = \ \((a\ b)\)\ c\)\)\)]],
  ";\n(2) identity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`e\)]],
  " exist, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(e\ a = \(a\ e\  = \ a\);\)\)]],
  "\n(3) each element ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\)]],
  " has inverse ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\^\(-1\)\)]],
  ", such that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ a\^\(\(-\)\(1\)\(\ \)\) = \(\(a\^\(-1\)\) a\  = \ 
          e\)\)]],
  ";"
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Examples of group: multiplication of unitary matrices; permutations of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\)]],
  " objects."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "ABELIAN Group:\nis a group with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ b\  = \ b\ a\)]],
  "."
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Examples of Abelian group: the set of integers Z = \
{...,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,...} with addition;  addition in Z",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\_n\)]],
  " for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\  = 2, 3, 4,  ... \)]],
  "; multiplication of Q",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`-\)]],
  "{0}."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "RING:\nClosed under two operations: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`+\)]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`*\)]],
  ".\n(1) ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`+\)]],
  " is an Abelian group;\n(2) ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`*\)]],
  " has identity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`e\)]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ \((b\ c)\)\  = \ \((a\ b)\)\ c\)]],
  ";\n(3) distribution law\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ \((b + 
              c)\)\  = \ \((a\ b)\)\  + \ \((a\ c)\), \[IndentingNewLine]\((a \
+ b)\)\ c\  = \ \((a\ c)\)\  + \ \(\((b\ c)\)\(.\)\)\)]]
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Examples of ring: addition and multiplication in Z; addition and \
multiplication of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\)]],
  " by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`n\)]],
  " matrices."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "INTEGRAL DOMAIN is a ring with\n(1) commutative for multiplication, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ b\  = \ b\ a\)]],
  ";\n(2) ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\ b\  = \ a\ c\)]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a \[NotEqual] 0\)]],
  " implies ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b = \(\(c\)\(.\)\)\)]]
}], "Theorem"],

Cell["\<\
Examples of integral domain: the set of integers Z; polynomials over integral \
domain; gaussian numbers. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "EUCLIDEAN DOMAIN is an integral domain such that\n\nWe can find  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\  = \ q\ b\  + \ r\)]],
  ",  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(b \[NotEqual] \ 0\)\(,\)\)\)]],
  "\nsuch that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`v(r)\  < \ v(b), \ 
      and\ \(v(a\ b)\)\  \[GreaterEqual] \ v(b)\)]],
  ".\nOf course ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a, b, q, r\)]],
  " all belong to D."
}], "Theorem"],

Cell["\<\
Examples of Euclidean domain:  the set of all integers Z; polynomials over a \
field; gaussian numbers.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "FIELD\n(1) + is an Abelian group;\n(2) ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`*\)]],
  " on F",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`-\)]],
  "{0} is an Abelian group;\n(3) has distribution law."
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Examples of field: addition and multiplication of rational number Q, real \
R, and complex number C;  Z",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\_p\)]],
  " for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`p\)]],
  " a prime;  Galois fields. "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Do you require me to remember all these definitions?  Yes.  At least roughly, \
if not precisely.\
\>", "Exercise"],

Cell["GCD, Unit, Prime, Square-Free, etc ", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We should know the additional concepts above.  \n\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`GCD(a, b)\)]],
  ":  in some sense largest element ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`c\)]],
  " such as  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`c | a\)]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`c | b\)]],
  ".  (largest in what sense?)   [ ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`c | a\)]],
  " means ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`c\)]],
  " can divide ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\)]],
  " evenly, that is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a = c\ q\)]],
  "]."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Unit:  a member that has (multiplicative) inverse.", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Prime:  a prime in an integral domain is a member that can not be factored \
into product of two other numbers, none of them is a unit.  Of course 0, 1 \
and other units are not prime.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Square-free:  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\)]],
  " is square-free if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a\)]],
  " does not have factor of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\^2\)]],
  " for some ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\)]],
  " not a unit.   That  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\^2 | c\)]],
  " is false unless ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\)]],
  " is a unit."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Algorithms", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
We are expected to be able to use the following algorithms in a pencil-paper \
calculation:

(0) Polynomial long division;
(1) Euclidean algorithm for GCD;
(2) Extended Euclidean algorithm;
(3) Garner algorithm for Chinese remainder problem;
(4) Square-free factorization algorithm.

We'll discuss the basis for the algorithms:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Euclidean algorithm",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],
  " is based on \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`GCD(a, b) = GCD(b, r)\)]],
  ", where\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(a\)\(=\)\)\)]],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`q\)]],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`b\)]],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`+\)]],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`r\)]],
  ".\nApply this recursively, we find remainder sequence ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`r\_0\  = \ a, \ r\_1 = b, \ r\_2 = r\)]],
  ", ..., ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`r\_n = 0\)]],
  ".\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`GCD(a, b)\)]],
  " is the last nonzero remainder.\n\nExtended Euclidean algorithm relies on \
the fact we have\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`r\_i = a\ s\_i + b\ t\_i\)]],
  "."
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Garner's Algorithm",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],
  "\n\nIt is based on the mix-radix expansion\n\n",
  Cell[BoxData[{
      \(TraditionalForm\`u = 
        v\_0\  + \ \(v\_1\) m\_0 + \ \(v\_2\) \(m\_0\) 
            m\_1 + \ \(v\_3\) \(m\_0\) \(m\_1\) m\_2\), 
    "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      \(TraditionalForm\`\(\(+\  ... \)\  + \ \(v\_\(\(n\)\(\ \)\)\) \(m\_0\) 
              m\_1 ... \)\ m\_\(n - 1\)\)}]]
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Familiarity with modular arithmetic (i.e. compute in Z",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\_p\)]],
  ") is a must."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Square-Free Factorization",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],
  "  expands as\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a(
          x)\  = \ \(\(a\_1\)(
              x)\)\ \(\(\(a\_2\)(x)\)\^2\) \(\(a\_3\)(x)\)\^3 ... \)]],
  "\n\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a(x)\)]],
  " is square free if and only if \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`GCD(a(x), a' \((x)\))\  = \ 1\)]],
  " (i.e. a unit).\nDivision and CGD calculation is used in square-free \
factorization procedure."
}], "Theorem"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Exercises:\n(1) compute ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`GCD(a \((x)\), b(x))\)]],
  " in Q and in Z",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\_5\)]],
  " using Euclidean and Exended Euclidean algorithm for the following \
polynomial:\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a(x)\  = \ 
        x\^3 + x\^2 - x - 1, \[IndentingNewLine]b(x)\  = \ x + 4\)]],
  "\nFor extended Euclidean algorithm, compute ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`s(x)\)]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`t(x)\)]],
  ".\n\n(2) Factor ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`a(x)\)]],
  " above with square free factorization algorithm in Q[x]. \n\n(3) Solve the \
Chinese remainder problem for \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`u\  \[Congruent] \ 
        1\ mod\ 2, \[IndentingNewLine]u\  \[Congruent] \ 
        3\ mod\ \ 5, \[IndentingNewLine]u\  \[Congruent] \ 2\ mod\ 7. \)]],
  "\nTo what extent the answer ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`u\)]],
  " is unique?"
}], "Exercise"],

Cell["A general principle of symbolic computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Solve the problem in a simpler domain (such as Z",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`\_p\)]],
  ") several times, and then reconstruct the original problem (by .e.g., \
Chinese remainder theorem).\n"
}], "Text"],

Cell["Format of Final Exam", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "4 questions:\n% 50 on ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (simple questions & programming)\n% 50 on symbolic algebra (including \
concepts and application of algorithms)"
}], "Text"]
},
